Given an integer array nums, return an array answer such that answer[i] is equal to the product of all the elements of nums except nums[i].
The product of any prefix or suffix of nums is guaranteed to fit in a 32-bit integer.
Example:
Input: nums = [1,2,3,4]
Output: [24,12,8,6]

Brute force
------------

class Solution {
    public int[] productExceptSelf(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] answer = new int[n];
        
        // Loop through each element in the array
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int currentProduct = 1;
            
            // Loop through all elements again to calculate the product
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                // Skip the current element itself
                if (i != j) {
                    currentProduct *= nums[j];
                }
            }
            
            // Assign the computed product to the answer array
            answer[i] = currentProduct;
        }
        
        return answer;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
		ProductOfArray solution = new ProductOfArray();
		int[] nums = {2,3,4,5};
		int[] result = solution.productExceptSelf(nums);
		System.out.print("Product of array except self: "+Arrays.toString(result));
	}
}

Outer Loop (i): Iterates through each index where a result needs to be calculated.
Inner Loop (j): Scans the entire array from scratch for every single index i.
Conditional Check (i != j): Skips multiplying the element when the inner loop index matches the outer loop index.

Optimal Way
-----------

public class ProductOfArray {
	public int[] productExceptSelf(int[] nums) {
		int n = nums.length;
        int[] answer = new int[n];
        
        // Step 1: Calculate prefix products
        // answer[i] will store the product of all elements to the left of index i
        answer[0] = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            answer[i] = answer[i - 1] * nums[i - 1];
        }
        
        // Step 2: Calculate suffix products on the fly and multiply
        // Maintain a running product of all elements to the right of index i
        int suffixProduct = 1;
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            answer[i] = answer[i] * suffixProduct;
            suffixProduct *= nums[i];
        }
        
        return answer;
    }
    
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		ProductOfArray solution = new ProductOfArray();
		int[] nums = {2,3,4,5};
		int[] result = solution.productExceptSelf(nums);
		System.out.print("Product of array except self: "+Arrays.toString(result));
	}
}

Time complexity: O(n) — two linear passes, no nesting.
Space complexity: O(1) extra space (not counting the output array) — you used a single suffixProduct variable instead of a second array. That's the stretch goal, done.

Pass 1 (prefix): answer = [1, 2, 6, 24]
  answer[0]=1
  answer[1]=1*2=2
  answer[2]=2*3=6
  answer[3]=6*4=24

Pass 2 (suffix, right to left):
  i=3: answer[3]=24*1=24,  suffixProduct=1*5=5
  i=2: answer[2]=6*5=30,   suffixProduct=5*4=20
  i=1: answer[1]=2*20=40,  suffixProduct=20*3=60
  i=0: answer[0]=1*60=60,  suffixProduct=60*2=120

Result: [60, 40, 30, 24]
